QUERN - QUIRKS AND GOTCHAS
Written at handoff, September 2026.

This file is a collection of all the rather strange things that I have discovered or had to work around while
building this app, so hopefully you do not need to go through the same pain I did.


READ THIS FIRST

Pushing to main is how you deploy to Azure. There is no staging environment but it would
be trivial to set up if you so desire.

Do not run /init, "First Time Startup", or "Wipe tasks" on production. They delete every task, and
tasks exist only in Quern.

Do not run flask db commands against production. The live alembic_version table points at a
revision that no longer exists. See "Schema changes" below.

Though its unlikely that you would, dont add any gunicorn workers. It is fine that this app runs on a single thread
because the concurrent user base is rather small, and many functions depend on being single threaded. For example,
if you added another worker, it would run the syncs several times a night.

Be careful with anything that triggers a full Zoho sync. The daily quota is 10,000 calls and a full
contract sync costs roughly 16,000.


HOW QUERN RELATES TO ZOHO

Zoho books is the contracting software that you are interfacing with. It is the authoritative state of truth
and Quern pulls all relevant data from it. Reads are local on quern because we sync the data from Zoho. Initially
I opposed this idea because it keeps the level of risk lower on the Quern side, but due to some of the needs of
the brokers and accountants, all zoho data had to be brought over into quern.

-The Contract primary key is Zoho's salesorder_id.

Some data exists only in Quern and cannot be rebuilt from Zoho: tasks, task templates, internal
notes, shipments, broker commissions, paid_date, the declined flag, the audit log, and users. This
is why database backups matter.

There is another Azure mount for the db, that azure points to. It is SQL-lite, simply because it was the simplest option at that time.
I suspect you could move it to a full fledged SQL db rather easily, but that was never a focus of mine

REPORTING TAGS: OFFICE AND P&L GROUP

This is the most intricate part of the Zoho integration.

Office is a line-item reporting tag, not Zoho's branch/location.
P&L Group is a separate line-item reporting tag. Office follows the
first broker's office. P&L Group follows the commodity. Staff sometimes say "origin PNL tag" when
they mean the Office tag.

Branch/location IDs and tag option IDs are separate namespaces; one cannot be derived from the
other. core/zoho.py has two hardcoded lists that do not agree:

get_locations() holds branch IDs and drives the office dropdown. S, SBC, SC, and Singapore have
empty branch IDs. Boulder and Head Office share a branch ID.

get_office_tag_option_id() maps office names to tag option IDs. FrontSeat, Shanghai, and Turkey are
missing, so contracts for those offices are submitted with no Office tag, silently. Boulder and Head
Office share a tag option ID.

Zoho's /reportingtags API does not expose tag option IDs. The office IDs were collected from
existing contracts using utility scripts/scrape_office_tags.py. A new office means scraping its ID
the same way and adding it to the map.

P&L Group option IDs are not hardcoded. They are read from each item's tags during item sync and
stored on Item.pnl_group_tag_option_id, so P&L tagging is only as current as the last item sync.

A PUT to a sales order replaces all of its tags. update_contract() fetches the existing contract
and re-sends its tags, merging Office and P&L Group separately. Without this, any edit erased them.

The office is matched by exact string. If the broker's office doesn't exactly match a key in the
tag map, no Office tag is sent. On sync, the Office tag name is read back into
Contract.location_name, falling back to Zoho's location_name.


ZOHO API BEHAVIOUR

The sales order list endpoint omits line items, tags, and custom fields, so every contract needs a
separate detail call (GET /salesorders/{id}).

The nightly contract sync is incremental: it filters on last_modified_time and only fetches changed
contracts. State is kept in SyncState under last_incremental_sync, with a 24-hour fallback on first
run. A bulk edit in Zoho that touches many records will still use a lot of quota.

Customer sync makes a detail call per customer (about 800 to 900) to get billing addresses. It used
to exceed the gunicorn timeout, which is why startup.sh sets --timeout 600.

Zoho refuses edits to sales orders that have been invoiced. Quern saves its local-only fields
before calling Zoho so a rejected push doesn't lose them.

A sales order's Zoho status stays "invoiced"; "closed" does not mean paid. Quern shows status with
this precedence: Declined (local), then Closed (paid_status is paid), then the Zoho status.

Employees come from a Zoho custom module, cm_employees, not from Books users. Only the first 200
are fetched. The office field's name varies between records, so the code tries several. An
employee is active unless the word "inactive" appears in cf_position.

Employee sync is not scheduled. It runs only from the dev panel or /init, and when it runs it
overwrites manual Active toggles and office edits made in Quern.

Deletions do not sync in either direction. A contract deleted in Zoho stays in Quern. Quern's admin
Delete removes the contract locally only, along with its shipments, commissions, notes, tasks, and
audit log entries, and does not touch Zoho.

Contracts created through Quern are numbered TEST-### by get_next_test_number(), which only scans
the 200 most recent sales orders. Unconfirmed whether this is intended; it looks like leftover test
code. Worth raising with Enrique.


FIELD MAPPING

Buyer is stored in cf_buyer as the buyer's name, not an ID. Quern matches it to a customer by exact
name, so a renamed or duplicate customer breaks the link.

Zoho has one shipment_date. The form's "end" date maps to it; the "start" field is not saved
anywhere. The variable names in the code are inverted relative to this (see the TODO in
core/zoho.py). cf_shipment_end_date is local-only despite the cf_ prefix; sending it to Zoho causes
an error, and sync must never overwrite it.

cf_item_contract_price is free text, such as "0.72 (WN26)". It must stay a string column.

The first broker is sent as salesperson_name, by name. The second broker goes to cf_split_broker,
not cf_co_broker (which is the external co-broker). cf_split_percentage is sent as a decimal, so
50% becomes 0.5.

Booking numbers are joined into the top-level reference_number.

location_id is sent when a contract is created but not when it is updated.

Item origin is cf_origin_location. An item's P&L Group is in its tags, not reporting_tags.

Custom field values equal to the text "none" are treated as empty.


ACCESS AND LOGIN

Logging in needs a Microsoft account and a matching row in the users table (emails matched
lowercase). Add users at /admin/users.

The user field called nationality actually means office. A non-admin with it set sees only that
office's contracts, plus any they are a broker on; with none set, they see everything. This is
enforced on the contracts list, item and counterparty pages, bulk edit, and the Excel export. It is
not enforced on the dashboard, contract pages opened by URL, PDF/DOCX downloads, or the CSV export.

Any logged-in user can toggle an employee's Active status. This is by design.

Dev, debug, and init routes are admin-only. There are no CSRF tokens; the session cookie is
SameSite=Lax.

The Entra client secret used for login expires. When it does, nobody can log in. Check the date in
Entra ID, App registrations, Quern, Certificates & secrets.


INFRASTRUCTURE

Azure App Service "quern" in resource group quern_group, Canada Central, B1, Python 3.11 on Linux.

One gunicorn worker (startup.sh), with APScheduler running in-process. Times are UTC: task
activation every minute, items at 00:00, customers at 00:05, contracts at 00:10, Power BI export at
00:15. The scheduler log is at /dev/scheduler/log. The Zoho access token is cached in memory, which
is fine for one worker.

The database is SQLite at /mnt/quern-data/quern.db on an Azure Files mount. Kudu's File Manager
doesn't reliably show /mnt; use SSH and find /mnt -name "quern.db" 2>/dev/null. Over SSH, the
deployed code lives under /tmp/<hash>/ with an antenv virtualenv.

Deployment runs through GitHub Actions on push to main. It logs in to Azure through the managed
identity oidc-msi-bd4c, which needs a federated credential for each repo and branch allowed to
deploy. Azure builds dependencies on the server.

Secrets (SECRET_KEY, the Zoho credentials, the Entra settings) live in App Service environment
variables. Never commit them.


SCHEMA CHANGES

The production migration history is broken, so add columns directly. Open SSH (App Service,
Development Tools, SSH) and run, for example:

python3 -c "import sqlite3; c=sqlite3.connect('/mnt/quern-data/quern.db'); c.execute('ALTER TABLE contracts ADD COLUMN example VARCHAR(100)'); c.commit(); print('ok')"

Then update core/models.py to match and deploy. The same pattern works for one-off data fixes.

This uses Python's built-in sqlite3 on purpose: importing app.py starts a second copy of the
scheduler inside the shell. If it reports "no such table", the path is wrong and it has created an
empty file there, which should be deleted.


REQUIREMENTS.TXT

The file is currently UTF-16 encoded, from a PowerShell pip freeze redirect. pip tolerates it but
other tools may not. Windows-only packages (pywin32) and heavy ones (pandas, numpy) have broken
deploys before. Review it by hand and prefer pinning only direct dependencies.


FRONT END

Select2 fires change events through jQuery, so native addEventListener('change') won't fire; use
jQuery .on('change'). Select2 also hides the original select element, which throws off anything
that measures element positions, such as the task spotlight.

The new-contract form restores saved input from sessionStorage only on ?restore=1, after a failed
submit.

The office field on both contract pages is filled by autoPopulateOfficeFromFirstBroker() when the
first broker changes.

To run the code, you may need to create a .env file. You can find all of the credentials on Azure. .env goes in the root.


KNOWN ISSUES AND DEAD CODE

/bulk_edit still uses the old status precedence.
second_broker_name is missing from the update_contract() payload.
The shipment "start" date is a dead input.
A Zoho push still fires when only local-only fields change. Harmless but wasteful.
item_detail renders every contract for a commodity with no row cap.
The contract_detail route passes both a Zoho-shaped dict and the model object to the template.
Legacy Query.get() calls are deprecated in SQLAlchemy 2.0.
create_user() in functions.py seeds a hardcoded list of users.
Power BI was abandoned. The scheduled export, core/powerbi_export.py, /contracts/export-csv, and the
dev panel buttons are leftovers and can be removed.
docs/security.md is a list of goals, not a description of what is implemented.


